WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.560
Himodialysis passes blood across a hollow fiber filter three times weekly, yet clinic overheads dominate its price. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 174. A patient sits in a clinic chair for four hours while blood cycles through a filter against treated water. The filter in the water are simple manufactured goods, produced at commodity scale. The full session price stays high because the service is locked inside staff facilities that run on fixed schedules and regulatory requirements.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:28.720
treatments.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.660
That separation between the physical process and the delivery system is where the opportunity

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:34.200
sits.

00:00:35.279 --> 00:00:40.039
Hemodialysis moves blood across a semi-permeable membrane so that waste diffuses into

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.059
a dialisate bath.

00:00:41.759 --> 00:00:45.779
The membrane, the fluid, and the basic pumping hardware are the physical core.

00:00:46.439 --> 00:00:51.859
In practice, the treatment occurs inside outpatient clinics that maintain nursing ratios, infection

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:54.319
control protocols, and real estate overhead.

00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:58.999
payment systems reimburse per session rather than per outcome or per home

00:00:58.999 --> 00:01:03.779
treatment, so the incentive structure rewards the existing site model. Early

00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:08.359
machines from the 1940s and 1950s were already functional, yet the chronic

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:12.539
care model that emerged after the Scribner shunt still roots most patients

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:16.439
through these central locations. Staffing accounts for a large share of

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:20.559
operating expense because each chair requires trained personnel for the entire

00:01:20.559 --> 00:01:26.019
duration. Real estate and scheduling constraints limit how many patients one facility can handle

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:31.379
without expanding square footage or extending hours. Regulatory rules on water quality,

00:01:31.679 --> 00:01:36.099
machine maintenance, and emergency response add further layers that clinics must satisfy

00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:41.379
regardless of patient volume. These layers feel normal inside the industry because the original

00:01:41.379 --> 00:01:47.019
chronic programs were built around hospital adjacent centers. The result is a service whose price

00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:51.679
reflects organization and compliance more than the cost of the filter or the treated water.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:56.579
When a clinic must keep a fixed number of chairs ready for staggered appointments, any

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:01.199
unused slots still incurs the same rent, utilities, and standby staff costs.

00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:06.859
Infection control rules require dedicated water treatment loops and regular validation testing

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:09.379
that cannot be skipped even on low volume days.

00:02:09.999 --> 00:02:14.859
Because reimbursement is tied to the clinic visit rather than the delivered filtration, there

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:18.139
There is little financial reward for shifting suitable patients elsewhere.

00:02:18.659 --> 00:02:22.999
Over decades, these practices became the default because they matched the hospital-based

00:02:22.999 --> 00:02:25.979
infrastructure that first made chronic dialysis possible.

00:02:26.885 --> 00:02:31.725
The supplied research sources trace the development of hollow fiber membranes and early machines,

00:02:32.265 --> 00:02:36.985
but they do not contain current commodity prices for regenerated cellulose or cellulose

00:02:36.985 --> 00:02:42.825
triacetate fibers, bulk dialisate salts, or the energy cost of water treatment at scale.

00:02:42.825 --> 00:02:47.265
material. Without those figures, a precise magic wand number cannot be calculated from

00:02:47.265 --> 00:02:51.805
the given material. The magic wand number is what the thing would cost if you paid

00:02:51.805 --> 00:02:57.045
only to arrange the raw atoms into the finished goods. Here that floor is simply not in the

00:02:57.045 --> 00:03:03.225
record, so I will not invent one. The sources do record that disposable sets in 1956 were

00:03:03.225 --> 00:03:08.825
listed near $60 per treatment. That figure is decades old, and it already included assembly

00:03:08.825 --> 00:03:12.585
and sterilization, so it is not a raw material floor either.

00:03:13.045 --> 00:03:17.825
The gap between any plausible raw material cost and today's session price therefore sits

00:03:17.825 --> 00:03:19.145
mainly in three places.

00:03:19.785 --> 00:03:24.265
First is the clinic real estate and the capital tied up in fixed schedules that leave chairs

00:03:24.265 --> 00:03:25.805
empty between patient blocks.

00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:31.585
Second is the labor cost of one-on-one nursing coverage required by current payment and safety

00:03:31.585 --> 00:03:31.985
rules.

00:03:32.565 --> 00:03:36.005
Third is the carrying cost of regulatory compliance and liability.

00:03:36.745 --> 00:03:39.705
Amortized across every treatment regardless of location.

00:03:40.385 --> 00:03:44.685
Home programs reduce the staffing and real estate portions because the patient or family

00:03:44.685 --> 00:03:46.325
supplies the time and space.

00:03:46.945 --> 00:03:51.365
The first principal's ratio is the finished price divided by that raw material floor.

00:03:51.845 --> 00:03:55.585
A ratio near one means the thing is already close to its physical limit.

00:03:56.125 --> 00:04:01.725
A large ratio means most of the price is design, process, organization, or rules rather

00:04:01.725 --> 00:04:05.765
than material, and that is where a from scratch rethink has room to work.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:10.925
The ratio itself cannot be stated as a single number, because the material floor is not

00:04:10.925 --> 00:04:11.425
supplied.

00:04:11.965 --> 00:04:16.425
What the sources make clear is that the dominant cost elements are organizational rather

00:04:16.425 --> 00:04:17.085
than material.

00:04:17.585 --> 00:04:19.165
That measures where the price sits.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:21.905
It is not a verdict on the people who built the clinics.

00:04:22.425 --> 00:04:26.925
Even if the hollow fiber bundle and dialisate salts could be purchased at bulk commodity

00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:31.065
rates, the clinic must still maintain separate spaces for machine storage.

00:04:31.065 --> 00:04:36.145
water purification, and emergency equipment that sit idle outside scheduled blocks.

00:04:36.765 --> 00:04:41.225
Nursing ratios are set by rules that assume every patient needs continuous observation,

00:04:41.625 --> 00:04:45.305
so one nurse cannot safely cover more than a handful of chairs at once.

00:04:45.925 --> 00:04:51.305
Compliance documentation, machine calibration logs, and water quality testing must be performed

00:04:51.305 --> 00:04:56.465
and archived whether the clinic runs at full capacity or half, spreading those fixed expenses

00:04:56.465 --> 00:04:58.825
over fewer treatments when utilization drops.

00:04:58.825 --> 00:05:03.765
shops. Each of these layers compounds because the payment model pays for the occupied chair

00:05:03.765 --> 00:05:09.263
rather than the filtration event itself. Stanley Schilden began training patients for

00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:15.963
unattended overnight home dialysis in London between 1961 and 1964, shifting the site from

00:05:15.963 --> 00:05:20.963
clinic to residence. That changed cut facility and staffing expense while keeping the same

00:05:20.963 --> 00:05:25.683
filter and fluid, and it removed the clinic overhead by moving overnight treatments into

00:05:25.683 --> 00:05:26.503
patients' homes.

00:05:27.123 --> 00:05:32.223
Sources record that early home programs reached roughly 40% of U.S. patients in the early

00:05:32.223 --> 00:05:36.023
1970s before Medicare policy shifts reverse the trend.

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:39.443
Those limits show that technology alone is not enough.

00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:44.763
Payment and regulatory structures must also change for the cost to move closer to the physical

00:05:44.763 --> 00:05:45.163
floor.

00:05:45.743 --> 00:05:50.443
A from-scratch redesign would first standardize compact machines that patients or caregivers

00:05:50.443 --> 00:05:52.103
can set up with minimal training.

00:05:52.103 --> 00:05:57.223
A compact machine would need built-in sensors that automatically pause treatment and alert

00:05:57.223 --> 00:06:00.963
a remote monitor if pressure or temperature drifts outside safe limits.

00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:06.143
Second, it would move water treatment and dialisate preparation to point-of-use cartridges

00:06:06.143 --> 00:06:09.783
that meet regulatory standards without central pharmacy oversight.

00:06:10.183 --> 00:06:15.443
Those cartridges would have to carry pre-steralized fluid paths whose integrity can be verified by

00:06:15.443 --> 00:06:20.103
the patient with a simple visual or electronic check rather than laboratory sampling.

00:06:20.103 --> 00:06:24.923
Third, it would adjust payment rules so that reimbursement follows the patient rather

00:06:24.923 --> 00:06:25.863
than the clinic chair.

00:06:26.463 --> 00:06:30.623
Reimbursement that follows the patient would let a verified home setup receive the same

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:35.843
or greater payment than a clinic session, removing the financial penalty that currently discourages

00:06:35.843 --> 00:06:36.343
the shift.

00:06:36.923 --> 00:06:42.103
Each step requires reliable low maintenance hardware, clear infection control protocols that

00:06:42.103 --> 00:06:46.783
families can follow, and regulatory acceptance of distributed water quality testing.

00:06:46.783 --> 00:06:52.163
The hard parts are infection risk, machine failure during unsupervised hours, and the

00:06:52.163 --> 00:06:56.003
need for rapid emergency response when something goes wrong outside a clinic.

00:06:56.563 --> 00:07:01.323
Infection risk remains the clearest barrier because any breach and sterile technique at

00:07:01.323 --> 00:07:04.163
home lacks the immediate backup of on-site staff.

00:07:04.703 --> 00:07:09.483
Machine failure during the night would require either redundant systems or a response protocol

00:07:09.483 --> 00:07:12.883
that reaches the patient faster than typical emergency services.

00:07:12.883 --> 00:07:17.963
diseases. Regulatory bodies would need to accept patient-performed water tests as equivalent

00:07:17.963 --> 00:07:23.283
to clinic grade validation, which demands documented proof that such tests catch the same

00:07:23.283 --> 00:07:28.857
contaminants with comparable reliability. When a medical service keeps its price high long

00:07:28.857 --> 00:07:33.957
after the core materials become inexpensive, the binding constraint is usually the rules

00:07:33.957 --> 00:07:39.237
that decide where and by whom the treatment can occur. A license can outlast the room that

00:07:39.237 --> 00:07:44.257
first made the treatment possible. Once the check can happen at the bedside, the remaining

00:07:44.257 --> 00:07:49.697
expense is permission, not the membrane. The open question is which payer or regulator

00:07:49.697 --> 00:07:55.057
will first test a reimbursement model that rewards verified home setups rather than clinic

00:07:55.057 --> 00:08:00.477
volume. That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost,

